// THE SURGE — SHORT CIRCUIT — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// THE SURGE as big beat / breaks — Prodigy and the Chemical Brothers at 140, A minor. The
// Surge is an electrical surge, so this is the riot version: the drama is filter sweeps,
// stops, sirens, zaps and huge drops. A game alternate of surge, selectable from the dev
// menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// The Surge's own break (x.xx.xx.x.xx.xx.) is the drum identity all the way through,
// fattened into a programmed breakbeat: the original clap figure (4 7 12) on a punchy
// snare, ghosts on a quieter layer, rolls, and a buzz-roll CRACKLE shorting the circuit at
// every seam. A distorted 303 plays the Surge bass line — verbatim in the drops, and as a
// figure (root octave . root . octave root .) under whatever chord a quote asks for. The
// riff is on a song-local distorted saw (driven inside the voice, so the per-bar filter
// sweeps close it), an acid squelch two octaves under, a plain square doubling it. The
// THEME (the riff at half speed) comes in on brass. A song-local SIREN (a triangle pitch
// LFO, ±5 semitones a bar) wails on the builds and the breakdown. The announcer shouts
// the game's own words: "SURGE!" on the one, and the stage's mission, "EVERYTHING AT
// ONCE! KEEP RUNNING!", landing its last word on the downbeat.
// 
//   1–4    IGNITION — the original loop (riff, bass, break) lowpassed 450→1400 Hz, the
//          break getting harder bar by bar (the cabinet-screen loop)
//   5–8    build — "SURGE!", siren, filter 2 k→7.5 k, snare 4s→8s→16s, riser, A then E
//          303 pedal, the riff stuttering, a short circuit into the drop
//   9–16   DROP ONE — "SURGE!", breakbeat, distorted 303 on the Surge line, the riff on
//          the saw an octave down + acid + square at the original pitch
//   17–24  THEME on brass + square over the riff; the 303 goes bar-rate (Am | F | G | E);
//          the power flickers on the last beat
//   25–40  CHANNEL SURF — four crushed windows over the Surge bed, each scratched in (its
//          first beat buffer-repeated, spinning up to pitch) and cut off, the riff's head
//          punching back in between:
//            25–28 FIELD SERVICE on the pixel square — tape stop
//            29–32 SPEED ZONE on the screamer — a one-beat hole
//            33–36 TERMINAL VELOCITY on the ice bell — tape stop
//            37–40 RHYTHM BANKRUPTCY on the toy piano — the power goes
//   41–48  BREAKDOWN — a lone acid line (its filter opening over 8 bars) and the siren;
//          CRYPT SHIFT on the theremin (41–44), FROST FORTRESS on the ice bell (45–48),
//          both through a closed filter over the THEME on the saw; the mission shouted
//   49–52  build two — the break rolls back in, riser, riff stutters, then its head
//          struck a semitone higher each beat over a 303 walking E F F#
//   53–68  DROP TWO — up a tone to B minor, "SURGE!"; the riff an octave up on the saw
//          with the screamer a second distorted layer; CARDBOARD KINGDOM (53–60) then
//          CORPORATE KOMBAT (61–68) answer it bar by bar on toy piano and brass, the
//          square an octave under; bar 68 the power goes down — everything tape-stops
//   69–80  EVERYTHING AT ONCE — back to A minor, "SURGE!": the riff, SPEED ZONE's
//          off-sixteenth riff as a hocket against it, CARDBOARD KINGDOM from 73, the
//          THEME on brass and the siren from 77; the heaviest break; the melody opening
//          from a 900 Hz lowpass over 69–76; the mission shouted into the loop point
// 
// THE EIGHT QUOTES (work/local/_surge-quotes.mjs, each at its own pitch — every hook
// already sits in the Surge's key): FIELD SERVICE 25–28 · SPEED ZONE 29–32 and 69–80 ·
// TERMINAL VELOCITY 33–36 · RHYTHM BANKRUPTCY 37–40 · CRYPT SHIFT 41–44 · FROST FORTRESS
// 45–48 · CARDBOARD KINGDOM 54–60 (even bars) and 73–80 · CORPORATE KOMBAT 62–68 (even
// bars). Drop two plays the two answers up a tone with the rest of the band.
// 
// THE LOOP: the cabinet screen loops bars 1–4; the stage loops bars 9–80 (123 s), so a clean
// run rarely hears the seam, and the mission's last word lands on bar 9's "SURGE!".
// 
// 80 bars at 140, 137 s. Written by work/local/_surge-remix-short-circuit.mjs through
// work/local/_remix-lib.mjs and work/local/_surge-quotes.mjs; re-running it rewrites this file.
//
// SPOT FX (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-surge.mjs — re-run it after a regeneration): the
// short circuits are real now, on the master. The power flickers (the mix gated in 1/32s) and
// goes dead on 8, 24 and 40; each channel-surf window is scratched in by a 1/16 Stutter of the
// whole mix on its downbeat (25, 29, 33, 37); 32 is a true hole; 28, 36 and 68 tape-stop as a
// lowpass closing a sixteenth at a time; 51–52 stutter into a hole. No TAPE snapshots.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
